automation, governance, risk,

00:01:38.860 --> 00:01:40.074
and compliance.

00:01:40.074 --> 00:01:41.990
And then I'm going to
dive a little bit deeper

00:01:41.990 --> 00:01:45.560
into Cloud Audit Logs, which
is a new service that we're

00:01:45.560 --> 00:01:48.540
launching and I'm really
quite excited about.

00:01:48.540 --> 00:01:52.730
So intuitive intelligent
management-- one

00:01:52.730 --> 00:01:55.470
of the great things
about the Cloud Platform

00:01:55.470 --> 00:01:58.320
is that you don't have to work
with multiple systems that

00:01:58.320 --> 00:01:59.860
don't talk to each other.

00:01:59.860 --> 00:02:02.560
You don't have to deal
with multiple vendors,

00:02:02.560 --> 00:02:06.150
a wide variety of systems
to provision resources.

00:02:06.150 --> 00:02:09.310
If you want to
generate a new VM,

00:02:09.310 --> 00:02:11.114
if you want to get
new storage, if you

00:02:11.114 --> 00:02:12.530
want to change
your networking, it

00:02:12.530 --> 00:02:15.780
can all be done from
within the same place.

00:02:15.780 --> 00:02:18.160
You can easily provision
resources with a single click,

00:02:18.160 --> 00:02:20.406
and more importantly,
deprovision them.

00:02:20.406 --> 00:02:22.280
So you no longer have
to deal with that world

00:02:22.280 --> 00:02:25.580
where adding new
resources requires a PO.

00:02:25.580 --> 00:02:28.730
It's really just a single click.

00:02:28.730 --> 00:02:31.590
We also want to ensure
that you can manage

00:02:31.590 --> 00:02:33.490
your environment your way.

00:02:33.490 --> 00:02:34.600
And what does that mean?

00:02:34.600 --> 00:02:37.190
What we know is that there
are lots of different people

00:02:37.190 --> 00:02:38.740
who are managing
these environments

00:02:38.740 --> 00:02:40.490
and you have different
preferences for how

00:02:40.490 --> 00:02:42.114
you're going to want
to work with this.

00:02:42.114 --> 00:02:45.140
Many of you may want to come
to this and work with a web UI

00:02:45.140 --> 00:02:48.140
like I demonstrated on the
last page, our Cloud Console.

00:02:48.140 --> 00:02:49.540
But many of you
are going to want

00:02:49.540 --> 00:02:51.990
to interact with
it through an API

00:02:51.990 --> 00:02:54.100
or through the command
line interface.

00:02:54.100 --> 00:02:56.120
And so we make sure
that all of our services

00:02:56.120 --> 00:02:59.640
expose their capabilities
through a REST API,

00:02:59.640 --> 00:03:01.870
through a command line
interface, as well as

00:03:01.870 --> 00:03:04.130
that Cloud Console.

00:03:04.130 --> 00:03:08.310
Now when problems happen,
and as they always do,

00:03:08.310 --> 00:03:12.120
they often happen when you're
not in front of your desktop.

00:03:12.120 --> 00:03:15.560
And so to complement or to
help deal with that situation,

00:03:15.560 --> 00:03:18.380
we provide a mobile management
and monitoring tool.

00:03:18.380 --> 00:03:21.340
So you can download this
mobile Cloud Console

00:03:21.340 --> 00:03:23.200
for Android or iOS.

00:03:23.200 --> 00:03:26.310
And it allows you to look
and see your environment.

00:03:26.310 --> 00:03:27.970
So you can see.

00:03:27.970 --> 00:03:29.930
You can do the
network monitoring.

00:03:29.930 --> 00:03:31.920
You can look at logs.

00:03:31.920 --> 00:03:34.030
If you want to interact
with individual resources,

00:03:34.030 --> 00:03:35.676
you can do that as well.

00:03:35.676 --> 00:03:38.050
For instance, you can look
and see this VM is causing you

00:03:38.050 --> 00:03:40.800
problems, and you can
stop it, delete it.

00:03:40.800 --> 00:03:43.200
And if you really want
to connect to it via SSH

00:03:43.200 --> 00:03:44.980
on your phone, you
can do that too.

00:03:47.710 --> 00:03:51.000
So that was really just
kind of a highlight overview

00:03:51.000 --> 00:03:53.950
of some of the management tools
that are available to you.

00:03:53.950 --> 00:03:57.050
I'm going to touch briefly
now on automation, governance,

00:03:57.050 --> 00:03:58.410
risk, compliance.

00:03:58.410 --> 00:04:01.810
And the first area I want
to focus on is automation.

00:04:01.810 --> 00:04:04.780
Now you've probably heard a
lot about this in the keynote

00:04:04.780 --> 00:04:06.430
this morning, how
Google has really

00:04:06.430 --> 00:04:09.050
been very active with
containers for a long time

00:04:09.050 --> 00:04:12.550
and have built out great
tools for managing that.

00:04:12.550 --> 00:04:15.500
And that has enabled us
to grow our infrastructure

00:04:15.500 --> 00:04:19.920
and our workloads much faster
than we've needed to grow

00:04:19.920 --> 00:04:21.544
our ops team to support that.

00:04:24.540 --> 00:04:27.150
And with that, based
on that experience,

00:04:27.150 --> 00:04:30.130
we developed Kubernetes, which
is an open source container

00:04:30.130 --> 00:04:32.000
orchestration system.

00:04:32.000 --> 00:04:33.830
And we've made it available.

00:04:33.830 --> 00:04:36.480
It's governed by an independent
foundation, is enjoying

00:04:36.480 --> 00:04:38.500
broad industry support.

00:04:38.500 --> 00:04:43.890
Importantly, we're embracing
the concept of multicloud

00:04:43.890 --> 00:04:46.330
as well as on-prem
VM bare metal.

00:04:46.330 --> 00:04:49.910
So you can use Kubernetes
in all these situations.

00:04:49.910 --> 00:04:52.460
And to make it easier for
you, particularly when

00:04:52.460 --> 00:04:55.460
working with GCP, we
provide Container Engine,

00:04:55.460 --> 00:04:59.630
which is a fully managed
and hosted Kubernetes system

00:04:59.630 --> 00:05:03.160
that allows you to very easily
create and deploy clusters

00:05:03.160 --> 00:05:04.877
with a single click.

00:05:04.877 --> 00:05:06.960
Now I'm not going to talk
too much more about this

00:05:06.960 --> 00:05:08.959
because you probably heard
a little bit about it

00:05:08.959 --> 00:05:09.740
at the keynote.

00:05:09.740 --> 00:05:11.750
But there's also a
great talk tomorrow

00:05:11.750 --> 00:05:15.700
at 11:00 AM going into much more
detail about Container Engine.

00:05:15.700 --> 00:05:19.400
So I would encourage
you to go to that.

00:05:19.400 --> 00:05:22.450
One thing that we know
is that as companies

00:05:22.450 --> 00:05:25.040
are moving to the cloud,
you're often either coming

00:05:25.040 --> 00:05:27.870
from on-prem or you're in a
multicloud environment, which

00:05:27.870 --> 00:05:31.370
means you're already using
tools for IT automation.

00:05:31.370 --> 00:05:33.720
And there's a large number
of open source tools

00:05:33.720 --> 00:05:36.370
out there that people are using.

00:05:36.370 --> 00:05:38.590
And we want you to
be able to continue

00:05:38.590 --> 00:05:41.570
to use the tools that you're
comfortable with as you

00:05:41.570 --> 00:05:42.960
come to the cloud.

00:05:42.960 --> 00:05:47.140
So we encourage our
engineers to participate

00:05:47.140 --> 00:05:49.960
in these open source projects
and to contribute to them

00:05:49.960 --> 00:05:54.420
to ensure that all these
tools work just as well on GCP

00:05:54.420 --> 00:05:59.560
or to ensure that you can
add GCP support to them.

00:05:59.560 --> 00:06:02.830
Now governance, this
is going to be quick,

00:06:02.830 --> 00:06:04.650
not because it's not
important-- because I

00:06:04.650 --> 00:06:06.108
know governance is
really important

00:06:06.108 --> 00:06:07.980
and one of our top
feature requests

00:06:07.980 --> 00:06:10.430
is around identity
and access management.

00:06:10.430 --> 00:06:14.340
But there's a whole talk
tomorrow at 2:15 solely focused

00:06:14.340 --> 00:06:16.840
on identity and
access management,

00:06:16.840 --> 00:06:19.920
talking about the capabilities
that we have there.

00:06:19.920 --> 00:06:21.490
And so I would
really encourage you

00:06:21.490 --> 00:06:25.310
to attend that talk as well.

00:06:25.310 --> 00:06:28.472
And now lastly in this
section, risk and compliance.

00:06:28.472 --> 00:06:30.180
This is an area that
I know is incredibly

00:06:30.180 --> 00:06:34.100
important to enterprises
and large organizations.

00:06:34.100 --> 00:06:37.410
And every year we participate
in independent audits

00:06:37.410 --> 00:06:39.650
of our infrastructure,
our services,

00:06:39.650 --> 00:06:41.540
our different operations.

00:06:41.540 --> 00:06:43.800
And the goal here is
to be comprehensive

00:06:43.800 --> 00:06:47.800
and to provide assurances
of the information security

00:06:47.800 --> 00:06:53.750
to help you feel
comfortable with this

00:06:53.750 --> 00:06:56.310
and to meet your security
and compliance needs.

00:06:56.310 --> 00:07:00.250
So you can see we've met
a number of cloud platform

00:07:00.250 --> 00:07:01.490
certifications.

00:07:01.490 --> 00:07:05.000
As a result of that, we're also
able to sign business associate

00:07:05.000 --> 00:07:08.730
agreements if you're working in
a HIPAA regulated environment.

00:07:08.730 --> 00:07:12.680
And then thinking more about
data security and privacy, just

00:07:12.680 --> 00:07:16.380
this past December, we launched
EU model contract clauses

00:07:16.380 --> 00:07:21.876
to help you with
managing your EU data.

00:07:21.876 --> 00:07:23.500
One of the important
things when you're

00:07:23.500 --> 00:07:24.916
thinking about
risk and compliance

00:07:24.916 --> 00:07:26.990
though is it's great
and very important

00:07:26.990 --> 00:07:28.870
that the platform has
these certification

00:07:28.870 --> 00:07:31.710
so that you can feel
comfortable with the platform

00:07:31.710 --> 00:07:32.810
that you're using.

00:07:32.810 --> 00:07:34.620
But it's also really
important for you

00:07:34.620 --> 00:07:38.210
to be able to know what
you're doing on the platform,

00:07:38.210 --> 00:07:40.410
because that plays a
major role there as well.

00:07:40.410 --> 00:07:43.920
And this ties in nicely
to my next topic,

00:07:43.920 --> 00:07:46.660
which are Cloud Audit Logs.

00:07:46.660 --> 00:07:48.600
So this is a new
service that we're

00:07:48.600 --> 00:07:50.570
providing to allow
you to understand

00:07:50.570 --> 00:07:54.780
who did what, where, and when
on Google Cloud Platform.

00:07:54.780 --> 00:07:56.730
And it's built on the
same proven and scalable

00:07:56.730 --> 00:07:59.250
infrastructure that powers
all of our other cloud logging

00:07:59.250 --> 00:08:01.607
services.

00:08:01.607 --> 00:08:03.690
And what's nice about this
is that the events that

00:08:03.690 --> 00:08:06.250
are captured in these logs,
they're available to you

00:08:06.250 --> 00:08:08.980
in the Cloud Console, as was
demonstrated in the keynote

00:08:08.980 --> 00:08:12.160
this morning, as well
alongside all the other logs

00:08:12.160 --> 00:08:15.120
in Stackdriver, which
you probably saw as well.

00:08:15.120 --> 00:08:17.560
And this requires no
configuration on your part.

00:08:17.560 --> 00:08:18.630
They're just there.

00:08:18.630 --> 00:08:21.280
They're available to you.

00:08:21.280 --> 00:08:23.990
And this has actually
been really embraced

00:08:23.990 --> 00:08:26.695
by a lot of our early customers
who have been using this.

00:08:26.695 --> 00:08:29.030
This quote from
Snapchat, from Subhash,

00:08:29.030 --> 00:08:31.710
who says, "Snapchat handles
sensitive user data,

00:08:31.710 --> 00:08:34.760
and it trusts Google Cloud
Platform with its storage.

00:08:34.760 --> 00:08:36.640
Cloud Audit Logging
provides us the ability

00:08:36.640 --> 00:08:38.840
to monitor this data across
a variety of resource

00:08:38.840 --> 00:08:41.320
types in near real
time, and for our teams

00:08:41.320 --> 00:08:43.929
to take timely
action if needed."

00:08:43.929 --> 00:08:45.230
So that's meeting our goals.

00:08:45.230 --> 00:08:48.506
This is what we want
you to be able to do.

00:08:48.506 --> 00:08:50.630
So I want to talk a little
bit more about the audit

00:08:50.630 --> 00:08:52.640
logs themselves, because this
is important to understand.

00:08:52.640 --> 00:08:54.970
There are actually two
different type of Audit logs.

00:08:54.970 --> 00:08:57.450
The first one is the
Admin Activity log.

00:08:57.450 --> 00:09:00.520
And this captures every
user action or API method

00:09:00.520 --> 00:09:03.730
that modifies the
configuration or metadata

00:09:03.730 --> 00:09:05.450
of a service or a project.

00:09:05.450 --> 00:09:09.410
So for instance when
you set the IAM policy,

00:09:09.410 --> 00:09:15.230
if you create a new project,
if you deploy a new App Engine

00:09:15.230 --> 00:09:17.870
application, all of
those get captured here.

00:09:17.870 --> 00:09:20.830
Those are actions that you're
taking that make a change

00:09:20.830 --> 00:09:22.440
to the service of the project.

00:09:22.440 --> 00:09:25.160
And these are always
on, for every project,

00:09:25.160 --> 00:09:26.640
no work required on your part.

00:09:26.640 --> 00:09:28.050
They're just available.

00:09:28.050 --> 00:09:29.796
And this is true
for all the services

00:09:29.796 --> 00:09:32.170
that have completed their
integration with the App Engine

00:09:32.170 --> 00:09:35.170
infrastructure-- the Audit
Logging infrastructure.

00:09:35.170 --> 00:09:36.950
And I say there
are no exemptions,

00:09:36.950 --> 00:09:40.060
and that means
basically everything

00:09:40.060 --> 00:09:44.240
that you do, all of
these actions get logged.

00:09:44.240 --> 00:09:47.350
Now the Data Access log
is a little bit different.

00:09:47.350 --> 00:09:50.170
This is where we capture
all of your interactions,

00:09:50.170 --> 00:09:52.040
whether they're
modifications or just

00:09:52.040 --> 00:09:59.580
reads, with user provided data
that's managed by the service.

00:09:59.580 --> 00:10:02.070
So as an example of
this, with BigQuery,

00:10:02.070 --> 00:10:05.190
when you import data
to a table a BigQuery,

00:10:05.190 --> 00:10:07.500
or if you export
data from BigQuery,

00:10:07.500 --> 00:10:10.660
or if you do a query within
BigQuery, all of these

00:10:10.660 --> 00:10:13.050
get captured in the
data access log.

00:10:13.050 --> 00:10:15.290
And we've broken them out
into two different logs

00:10:15.290 --> 00:10:17.770
because they have
different use cases.

00:10:17.770 --> 00:10:19.910
And also the Data
Access log tends

00:10:19.910 --> 00:10:23.060
to be a lot higher volume
than the Admin Activity log.

00:10:23.060 --> 00:10:27.500
So we don't want there to be
one to flood the other out.

00:10:27.500 --> 00:10:31.942
I would say the model for
these are mostly opt in.

00:10:31.942 --> 00:10:33.400
For the Data Access
logs, you would

00:10:33.400 --> 00:10:35.190
have to explicitly turn it on.

00:10:35.190 --> 00:10:37.450
Although right now the
BigQuery audit logs

00:10:37.450 --> 00:10:40.280
are on by default for
everybody because there's

00:10:40.280 --> 00:10:42.530
been such positive
feedback about the value

00:10:42.530 --> 00:10:47.400
that they have in understanding
your utilization of BigQuery.

00:10:47.400 --> 00:10:48.850
And there's one last point here.

00:10:48.850 --> 00:10:50.440
So I had said with
the Admin Activity

00:10:50.440 --> 00:10:52.250
logs there were no exemptions.

00:10:52.250 --> 00:10:54.750
But with the Data
Access logs, we

00:10:54.750 --> 00:10:58.300
do have the capability for
you to exempt certain service

00:10:58.300 --> 00:11:00.910
accounts from data
access logging.

00:11:00.910 --> 00:11:03.620
Now this isn't a self-serve
process at the moment.

00:11:03.620 --> 00:11:05.120
But the idea here
is that you really

00:11:05.120 --> 00:11:09.490
want to understand when your
users are looking at data,

00:11:09.490 --> 00:11:13.510
not necessarily when
your high QPS application

00:11:13.510 --> 00:11:14.740
is querying the data.

00:11:14.740 --> 00:11:17.740
So if you have a known,
trusted application running

00:11:17.740 --> 00:11:19.940
as a service account,
you can tell us that

00:11:19.940 --> 00:11:23.530
and we will exempt
that from logging.

00:11:23.530 --> 00:11:25.600
As I said going
back to governance

00:11:25.600 --> 00:11:27.545
as we were
introducing IAM, there

00:11:27.545 --> 00:11:31.410
are a number of access roles
that are relevant here.

00:11:31.410 --> 00:11:32.840
There's the Logs
Reader role which

00:11:32.840 --> 00:11:35.730
grants you access to
the Admin Activity logs.

00:11:35.730 --> 00:11:37.830
There's the Private
Logs Reader role.

00:11:37.830 --> 00:11:39.320
And this is a
separate permission

00:11:39.320 --> 00:11:42.330
that grants you access to the
Data Access logs independent

00:11:42.330 --> 00:11:43.580
of the Admin Activity logs.

00:11:43.580 --> 00:11:45.750
And we do this because
the Data Access

00:11:45.750 --> 00:11:47.910
logs contains information
about your access

00:11:47.910 --> 00:11:49.440
to user provided data.

00:11:49.440 --> 00:11:52.440
So often there's a different
level of sensitivity about

00:11:52.440 --> 00:11:54.570
who gets to see those logs.

00:11:54.570 --> 00:11:56.000
There's also a
Config Writer role

00:11:56.000 --> 00:12:00.170
which allows you to change or
configure the export settings

00:12:00.170 --> 00:12:02.390
with the logging system
in that you may not

00:12:02.390 --> 00:12:05.670
want to have the person who
is interacting with those logs

00:12:05.670 --> 00:12:08.195
also being able to change that.

00:12:08.195 --> 00:12:10.070
And then lastly there's
this Logs Writer role

00:12:10.070 --> 00:12:11.944
which I put up here
because it's actually not

00:12:11.944 --> 00:12:14.700
relevant to Audit logs,
because Audit logs are

00:12:14.700 --> 00:12:17.260
special with respect to
the other type of logs

00:12:17.260 --> 00:12:19.750
that we collect in
that they're immutable

00:12:19.750 --> 00:12:24.140
and nobody can write to them
except for Google services.

00:12:24.140 --> 00:12:26.860
So App Engine can
write to these,

00:12:26.860 --> 00:12:30.650
but you as a user can't call
the logging API an put logs

00:12:30.650 --> 00:12:33.610
into the Audit log streams.

00:12:33.610 --> 00:12:35.750
So where do we stand right now?

00:12:35.750 --> 00:12:38.000
This is where we are
with Admin Activity logs.

00:12:38.000 --> 00:12:39.630
We have a number of
services in beta.

