00:38:39.860 --> 00:38:42.260
there was an automated
compliance run

00:38:42.260 --> 00:38:46.710
for our particular CVE, which
had led to this compliance

00:38:46.710 --> 00:38:48.000
failure.

00:38:48.000 --> 00:38:51.320
And that compliance
failure was detected

00:38:51.320 --> 00:38:54.990
the moment the compliance
run was performed.

00:38:54.990 --> 00:38:57.010
And then it was
automatically remediated

00:38:57.010 --> 00:38:59.080
based on the policy
that you have set up

00:38:59.080 --> 00:39:01.710
for your public cloud offerings.

00:39:01.710 --> 00:39:05.850
And that remediation job
completed successfully.

00:39:05.850 --> 00:39:08.350
Subsequent to that,
another compliance run

00:39:08.350 --> 00:39:13.360
was made to ensure that
this compliance remediation

00:39:13.360 --> 00:39:16.430
laws completed successfully
and it did resolve

00:39:16.430 --> 00:39:19.310
the problem that had triggered
compliance failure to begin

00:39:19.310 --> 00:39:20.160
with.

00:39:20.160 --> 00:39:23.520
This policy or this mechanism
is applicable not only

00:39:23.520 --> 00:39:27.320
to custom compliance
policies but all types all

00:39:27.320 --> 00:39:29.560
industry standards
like HIPPA, SOX.

00:39:29.560 --> 00:39:32.230
And they come out of
the box, the support

00:39:32.230 --> 00:39:34.100
for all the industry
standard compliances.

00:39:34.100 --> 00:39:36.810
For custom compliances, you
can build custom compliance

00:39:36.810 --> 00:39:40.580
policies for CVEs and
beyond, and any other kind

00:39:40.580 --> 00:39:42.340
